						<div class="position-relative">
							<div class="collapse mb-0 card-text" id="collapseReview5170249">
								If you’ve played DnD before you probably met that one person that built a whole world on their own and now can’t stop talking about it. I know someone like that, and I’ve been that person before as well. Esoteric Ebb is clearly made by just such a person. World-building might be a little overrated, but I can’t help but marvel at the passion that it takes to craft a well-constructed world. That is where Esoteric Ebb shines most. If you like world-building you will like Esoteric Ebb, its world is overflowing with detail and passion.<br><br>One thing that disappointed me though was Esoteric Ebb’s take on politics, especially coming from a game clearly inspired by Disco Elysium. The game tells the story of an election, the first for the state of Norvik. It’s a state that has many ideologies inside and outside trying to guide its future. It tells you of capitalism creeping into the system, as well as the nationalism of old clinging to power. The game is quite precise in identifying these problems, showing how they manifest, and how they could harm this dream of a democratic state.<br><br>Where Esoteric Ebb falters is in offering an answer to these problems. The game ends with the election. It leaves on the hopeful note that democracy will overcome all these problems because people are inherently good. I can sympathize with that idea, who couldn’t. I love democracy and I hope you do too. And I do believe in the good in people. But the problem is that democracy, as we have it now, doesn’t allow that goodness to manifest.<br><br>Just think about how far you, as an individual, are removed from the democratic process. You get to vote once in a while, maybe even join a party and vote for your representative there. But that’s it. Between elections you have little to no say in the politics that affect you. The current system of parliaments and nations is simply not participatory enough to be truly democratic.<br><br>I don’t know the perfect way to organize a democratic future, and I don’t expect Esoteric Ebb to offer one either. That said, I think it is harmful to be complacent within the current system. It is simply not enough to say “Everything will be alright because I can vote”. It is not enough to say “All parties are bad but at least I can vote for the best one”. If you love democracy, you should want <em>more</em> than that. And Esoteric Ebb simply doesn’t offer that.
