							<div class="collapse mb-0 card-text" id="collapseReview5182412">
								This is an incredibly well made gem that pushes the limits of what can be done with RPG Maker MV, functioning as a nearly perfect conjuring of style and substance that creates a lovely experience. <br><br>To begin, I found the gameplay loop of <em>Zelle</em> to be really enjoyable. The game mostly functions as a simple point and click dungeon crawler, with "combat" sections that consist of quick time events where the only way to vanquish a foe is to press the correctly colored bead before the enemy grabs you within the designated time limit. It's simple enough on its face, where all you truly have to do to defeat an enemy is to click one button. And yet as you play through, the game almost ambushes you with creative usages of that system in order to keep you on your toes. If you don't pay attention and find an enemy in your way, you have to be ready to find the correct color to exorcise your foe. The game's atmosphere really ends up complimenting that agitation you experience from the combat by being so thick and brooding at every turn. Things change fast in the castle you're stuck in, and you can turn around and find an environment that once looked calm and tranquil then end up being wildly unnerving. <br><br>What I just described happens to only be one part of the game, which ends up shifting around the halfway point quite radically. That type of experimentation really aligns with my tastes, as it manages to masterfully reset your interest by the point the system described above might end up losing its novelty. It's a great lesson in how to properly pace a game's runtime, as the gameplay of a game shouldn't be treated as a means to push the narrative forward, with no desire to make the actual gameplay experience creative and compelling. This is a problem you see with a lot of RPG Maker projects, and perhaps the most contentious part of discussion surrounding these games (despite the fact that the engine itself is not at fault for the way people produce games within it, but I digress). <em>Zelle</em> avoids this completely with how dedicated the developers are to making the gameplay loop actually feel like a progressive part of the game's evolution as you play through it. <br><br>That sort of creative usage of different forms in a game can very easily become an unfocused mess of ideas which make you think the developer had only wanted to put as many ideas as they possibly could inside the game, but it ends up feeling more like a cohesive and vibrant collage of multiple ideas which work in tandem together to create an incredibly unique experience. I don't really have anything bad to say about <em>Zelle</em> at all for that reason. It's a lovely, distinctive, and eccentric game with a storybook charm that makes it perfect for playing on a late night before you go to bed.<br><br><strong>Recommended.</strong>
							</div>
